# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own
# Project_Plan_Export_Agent, a standalone action -- export the current
# Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog or Proposal Response as an
# illustrative Microsoft Project / ProjectLibre interchange-format (MSPDI
# XML) project plan, from whatever is already saved on disk. NO
# re-analysis, NO backlog/proposal regeneration, and the saved
# portfolio_backlog.json/proposal_response.json is never modified. This is
# deliberately NOT portfolio_backlog.hocon's/proposal_response_pipeline.
# hocon's own pipeline run with its early steps skipped -- same reasoning
# as process_subprocess_regenerate.hocon's own header comment, and the
# same standalone shape as process_visio_export.hocon.
#
# Domain-agnostic by design, mirroring process_visio_export.hocon's own
# Visio_Export_Agent_Main: a bare "export a project plan" doesn't say
# which artifact, so this front-man determines schema_type itself from the
# user's own request wording, rather than requiring the router to have
# already disambiguated it.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Exports the current Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog or Proposal Response as an illustrative Microsoft Project / ProjectLibre (MSPDI XML) project plan, from whatever is already saved on disk -- no re-analysis, no regeneration.",
        "tags": ["portfolio", "proposal", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Export the portfolio backlog as a project plan.",
            "Give me an MS Project file for our proposal response."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Project_Plan_Export_Agent_Main",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I export the current Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog or Proposal Response as a real Microsoft
Project / ProjectLibre interchange-format (MSPDI XML) project plan, from whatever is already saved
on disk. I always also generate a PERT/CPM network diagram alongside it, from the same scheduled
tasks. I never re-run analysis, backlog/proposal generation, or review, and I never modify the
saved data itself. Tell me to export the backlog or the proposal response as a project plan.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's project-plan-export request."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You export the current Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog or Proposal Response as an illustrative
Microsoft Project / ProjectLibre (MSPDI XML) project plan, from whatever is already saved on disk.
You NEVER re-run analysis, backlog/proposal generation, or review, and you NEVER modify the saved
portfolio_backlog.json/proposal_response.json itself -- that is every other network's job, on a
separate request.

The exported plan's durations are an ILLUSTRATIVE planning estimate computed only at export time
(Portfolio Backlog: one working day per WSJF story point; Proposal Response: parsed from each
implementation_plan phase's own free-text timing, falling back to a nominal estimate when
unparseable). Resource usage is derived directly from each backlog activity's calculated resourcing
(or caller-supplied role overrides when explicitly stated). If asked, say this plainly -- it is not
a committed schedule.

# DETERMINE schema_type FIRST
Check whether "inquiry" itself names a domain:
- If it explicitly says "backlog" / "portfolio backlog" / "epics" / "WSJF", schema_type =
  "portfolio_backlog".
- If it explicitly says "proposal" / "proposal response" / "RtP" / "response", schema_type =
  "proposal_response".
- Otherwise -- a genuinely generic "export a project plan" with no artifact named -- ask the user
  to clarify whether they mean the portfolio backlog or the proposal response, rather than
  guessing. Do NOT call export_project_plan without a determined schema_type.

# OPTIONAL PLANNING ASSUMPTIONS (extract from the user's own wording -- never fabricate any of
# these when not stated)
- resources: if the user names roles/people with a % allocation (e.g. "Project Manager (50%)",
  "ServiceNow Engineer at 100%"), pass resources=[{"name": "Project Manager", "allocation_percent":
  50}, ...]. Only include entries the user actually named -- never invent a role or guess a
  percentage that wasn't stated. If no allocation is named at all, omit resources entirely rather
  than guessing 100% for everyone.
- target_duration_weeks: if the user states a target length (e.g. "running over 3 months", "a
  6-week project"), convert to weeks (approximate months as 4 weeks each, and say so if asked) and
  pass it. Omit if no target length was stated.
- holiday_calendar: if the user says to honor UK/British public or bank holidays, pass
  holiday_calendar="uk". Only "uk" is supported -- if a different country's holidays are
  requested, say plainly that only a UK calendar is supported today and proceed without one
  (weekends-only) unless the user says otherwise, rather than silently ignoring the request with no
  explanation.

# SEQUENCE
1. Determine schema_type as above. If genuinely ambiguous, ask a clarifying question and stop --
   do not call any tool.
2. Call export_project_plan with that schema_type, plus start_date/resources/
   target_duration_weeks/holiday_calendar for any of those the user's own request actually stated
   (per the OPTIONAL PLANNING ASSUMPTIONS section above) -- omit any that weren't stated rather
   than guessing a value.
3. If the result starts with "ERROR", relay it verbatim -- there is no saved backlog/proposal
   response to export yet; suggest creating one first (e.g. "create an Enterprise Agile Portfolio
   Backlog from the saved requirements" / "draft a proposal response using the saved requirements
   summary").
4. If the result contains a "WARNING" (the plan overran a stated target_duration_weeks), relay
   that warning's own text verbatim -- do not soften, omit, or paraphrase it into a confident
   on-schedule claim.
5. Otherwise, report in plain language that the project plan was exported, the exact saved path
   from the tool's own result, and restate briefly that durations/sequencing are an illustrative
   planning estimate, not a committed schedule. The tool's own result also mentions a PERT/CPM
   network diagram generated alongside it -- relay that path too, in the same reply.

Plain prose only in your final reply -- no raw XML, tool names, or internal field names.
""",
            "tools": ["export_project_plan"]
        },

        {
            "name": "export_project_plan",
            "function": {
                "description": "Exports the current saved Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog or Proposal Response as an MSPDI project plan file, plus a companion PERT/CPM network diagram generated from the same scheduled tasks.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "schema_type": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["portfolio_backlog", "proposal_response"],
                            "description": "Which saved artifact to export a project plan from."
                        },
                        "start_date": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional ISO \"YYYY-MM-DD\" project start date. Defaults to today if omitted."
                        },
                        "resources": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "Optional list of named resources with their % allocation, extracted from the user's own wording -- never fabricated.",
                            "items": {
                                "type": "object",
                                "properties": {
                                    "name": {"type": "string", "description": "The role or person's name, as the user stated it."},
                                    "allocation_percent": {"type": "number", "description": "0-100, as the user stated it."}
                                },
                                "required": ["name", "allocation_percent"]
                            }
                        },
                        "target_duration_weeks": {
                            "type": "number",
                            "description": "Optional target project length in weeks, if the user stated one (approximate months as 4 weeks each). A post-hoc overrun check only -- never compresses the schedule to fit."
                        },
                        "holiday_calendar": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["uk"],
                            "description": "Only \"uk\" is supported. Set when the user asks to honor UK/British public or bank holidays."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["schema_type"]
                }
            },
            "class": "project_plan_export_tool.ExportProjectPlanCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
